{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Now Israel loved Joseph more than all his sons, because he was a son of old age to him.</b> Even though it is said (Genesis 37:2), ״וְהוּא נַעַר אֶת בְּנֵי בִלְהָה״ — that he would act in a youthful manner like them — nevertheless Jacob loved him, because when he was with him he conducted himself like an elder, well-versed, and his portion was seemly, and he did not know his conduct with his brothers. Therefore it says ״כִּי בֶן זְקֻנִים הוּא לוֹ״ — in the word ״לוֹ״ it indicates that specifically to him he was a son of old age and not to his brothers, because when he was with them he acted in a youthful manner like them, and when he was with his old father he acted in the manner of old age. And perhaps he did not wish to deviate from the custom and knew how to conduct himself both with the youth and with the elders; therefore it says ״הוּא לוֹ״." "There are those who say that whatever he learned from Shem and Eber he handed on to him. And why did he not hand on to all his sons what he had learned? Surely it is because all his sons rejected the wisdoms and did not attach themselves to Jacob to hear from him; but Joseph — ״בֶּן זְקֻנִים הוּא לוֹ״, who constantly turned toward Jacob and yearned to hear from his mouth what he had learned from Shem and Eber; therefore he loved him. And its Targum is ״בַּר חַכִּים הוּא לוֹ״, for בִּישִׁישִׁים חָכְמָה. This is what they said (Genesis Rabbah 84:8) that the splendor of his countenance resembled his, for ״חָכְמַת אָדָם תָּאִיר פָּנָיו״ (Ecclesiastes 8:1). And there is a hint here to what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 40b) — they all answered and said: study is great, for it leads to practice. Thus it mentioned here the perfection of Joseph in the verse ״הוּא הָיָה רֹעֶה אֶת אֶחָיו בַּצֹּאן״ (Genesis 37:2) — to exclude all forms of forbidden shepherding — and even so, he loved him only on account of ״כִּי בֶן זְקֻנִים הוּא לוֹ״, a wise son, for study is great, as stated." "<b>And he made him a striped tunic.</b> It appears that after Reuben had already disturbed his father's bed, the birthright was taken from him, and now he handed it on to Joseph. And on account of this he made him a striped tunic, because the service belonged to the firstborn, and the firstborn was a priest to El Elyon; therefore he made this tunic for honor and splendor — it resembles the priestly garments, which included a tunic of chequered weave. And perhaps it is on this account that it says ״כִּי בֶן זְקֻנִים הוּא לוֹ״ — as if he were the elder and the firstborn among all his sons; therefore it says ״לוֹ״, for in truth he was not the eldest among them all. And the doubling that they said — ״הֲמָלֹךְ תִּמְלֹךְ עָלֵינוּ, אִם מָשׁוֹל תִּמְשֹׁל בָּנוּ״ (Genesis 37:8) — points to this, for the firstborn takes a double portion in everything he is found, and the kingship belongs to him alone, as it is written (Genesis 49:3) ״רְאוּבֵן בְּכוֹרִי אַתָּה, יֶתֶר שְׂאֵת וְיֶתֶר עָז״ — that is, kingship. And this will be explained nearby in the verse ״מַה בֶּצַע״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {1 "The verse cited as Ecclesiastes 8:13 (״חָכְמַת אָדָם תָּאִיר פָּנָיו״) is actually Ecclesiastes 8:1 in standard enumeration; the citation is translated exactly as the author wrote it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:20.156446029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}